  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/moenkhausia-simulata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Among themselves, these tetras are quite robust. An aquarium as large as possible and well structured, with as many specimens as possible, ensures that aggressive actions do not result in fin damage.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/moenkhausia-simulata-2/",
      "html": "<p>The sex difference is particularly interesting in this species. The fish are currently 4–5 cm long and obviously sexually mature. The adipose fin of the males is clearly bigger than that of the females, and it is rusty-black in the dominant male in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/moenkhausia-simulata-2/",
      "html": "<p>A very rare species of tetra was recently imported from Peru. In all probability it is Moenkhausia simulata; however, the new import differs slightly in coloration from the previously known M. simulata. All other tetra species with a pattern of several horizontal stripes (Astyanax lineatus, A. kullanderi, A. superbus, Bario spp., Hollandichthys spp., Hyphessobrycon hexastichos, Markiana nigripinnis, Moenkhausia agnesae, M. latissima, M. rara, M. simulata and Pseudochalceus lineatus) either look completely different and/or have a completely different distribution area. Possibly, the imported fish is a scientifically undescribed species very similar to M. simulata.</p>"
    }
  ],
